4. CRITIQUE
---------------------------------------------------------------
What was correct and useful:
- It respected the SOW's architecture rule. USER is only
  referenced, not redefined, which is exactly what Section 4
  requires.
- The composite primary keys on POST_VOTE and REPLY_VOTE enforce
  one vote per user at the database level. That handles vote
  gaming without relying on Java code to remember the check.
- MODERATION_ACTION is append-only with moderator_id, reason,
  and timestamp. That lines up with the SOW's auditable log
  requirement and with what Subsystem 7 will need to read.

What I changed and why:

Problems, risks, or things that are wrong:
- pinned_by is supposed to be Staff only, but nothing in the
  schema enforces that. It has to be checked in the service layer.
- FLAG has both post_id and reply_id. Nothing stops a flag from
  pointing to a reply that belongs to a different post. That
  needs a check constraint or service validation.
- value on the vote tables is a smallint with no CHECK
  constraint, so a bad request could store +50.
- The denormalized counters (vote_score, reply_count) can drift
  under concurrent writes unless every update happens in the
  same transaction as the vote or reply.
- No ON DELETE behavior or indexes are specified. Search needs a
  full-text index, and created_at and last_activity_at need
  indexes for sorting.
- reputation in FORUM_USER_SETTINGS may overlap with what Shared
  Core stores. We need to confirm ownership with Subsystem 1.
- Removed posts don't record who removed them on the row itself.
  That is only recoverable by joining through MODERATION_ACTION.

Outdated or insecure: nothing outdated. The main security gap is
that role-based rules (pinning, moderation access) live outside
the schema and must be enforced in code.

